\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {V}{\ignorespaces Audited probe tables (300 items). Stage A: frozen Part I winner. Stage B: evidence-trained checkpoint. P2A is numerically identical to P1A in both stages, for the reason given in the text.}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:stageab}{{V}{4}{}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Headline accuracy beside accuracy including uncertain, for every headline arm comparison in Part II. The accounting rule, in plain words: headline accuracy counts every item the arbitration labels uncertain as wrong; accuracy including uncertain scores those items by the model's own screen instead, so the two bars for an arm differ only in how its abstentions are scored. Where a gap between arms appears in the blue bars and vanishes or inverts in the orange bars, the gap was a movement in how often the model committed, not in how often it was right. Panel B is the worked example: the evidence arm's headline gain of $+0.2467$ coexists with falsified accuracy of 0 of 148. Panel D is Arm W read under fitted accounting; at plain 0.5 thresholds the same arm's paired difference flips sign, as the text reports. Panel E shows the scale probe's collapse running the other way, into abstention: at 10,040 training items the model is uncertain on 274 of 300 items. Uncertain counts are printed under each arm.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:money}{{2}{5}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {VI}{\ignorespaces Stage B per-class reads (300 probe items; gold 152 pristine, 148 falsified). Choice recall scores the choice head alone; headline recall scores the arbitrated verdict. The pair-only arm's correct falsified choices are all nulled by arbitration; the evidence arm's choice head almost never chooses falsified.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:stagebclass}{{VI}{5}{}{table.6}{}}
